[Event "Chapter 1: Amateur Main Line (6.Be3?!) / Study 1: White Plays 7.Bd3, Nxc6 or f3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/34f13732-7c86-4f53-a808-071ebc0d161e/studies/b6f09bdf-0abd-472d-be7d-17be08a58e73/lines"]

1.e4 {The 4 Knights Sicilian is exactly what the name says: a Sicilian in which all 4 knights get developed before anything else. This can lead to positional and "boring" games, but usually makes for an interesting fight as there is a lot of control over the center early. In Chapter 1, we'll take a look at some of the most common moves at amateur level.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The first knight comes to f3. We'll develop ours to c6.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. These are the starting moves of the Open Sicilian, the most popular Sicilian variation. Almost 50% of games feature the Open Sicilian. That's more than 60 million games in the database! Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop to f6 and attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends, and now we have several choices, but 5...e6 is the Four Knights Sicilian. Our immediate plan is to continue developing quickly and actively with moves such as ...Bb4 and prepare the ...d5 break. Generally speaking, the ...d5 pawn break is a major goal for the Black pieces in the Sicilian.} 5...e6 6.Be3 {Many White players may already be out of book here and start playing "soft" moves such as Be3, which happens 25% of the time. Although it looks like a reasonable developing move, it does nothing to quell our aggressive central intentions of ...Bb4 and ...d5. So we'll go for ...Bb4, and the e4-pawn is already under attack.} 6...Bb4 7.Bd3 {The best option to at least develop is Bd3, protecting the pawn that way. It's played 25% of the time. We have an easy decision to make, as White has done nothing to prevent ...d5.} 7...d5 8.exd5 {If they capture, we don't want to recapture with the pawn and leave ourselves with an IQP. Taking with the knight is more practical. It puts pressure on White's weak pieces.} 8...Nxd5 9.Nxc6 {White can trade. We'll recapture. This trade is fine, as they don't have an active knight in the center anymore.} 9...bxc6 10.Bd2 {The bishop will have to retreat to d2 at some point to keep everything together. We'll just castle. We can allow White to trade on d5 as that would bring our pawns back together.} 10...O-O 11.O-O {They just castle instead.} 11...Rb8 {The b-file is much better for our rook than the a-file. A possible maneuver is ...Qc7, ...Bd6 and ...Rd8. It is an equal position because our centralized knight helps us defend against White's potential attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Amateur Main Line (6.Be3?!) / Study 1: White Plays 7.Bd3, Nxc6 or f3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/34f13732-7c86-4f53-a808-071ebc0d161e/studies/b6f09bdf-0abd-472d-be7d-17be08a58e73/lines"]

1.e4 {The 4 Knights Sicilian is exactly what the name says: a Sicilian in which all 4 knights get developed before anything else. This can lead to positional and "boring" games, but usually makes for an interesting fight as there is a lot of control over the center early. In Chapter 1, we'll take a look at some of the most common moves at amateur level.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The first knight comes to f3. We'll develop ours to c6.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. These are the starting moves of the Open Sicilian, the most popular Sicilian variation. Almost 50% of games feature the Open Sicilian. That's more than 60 million games in the database! Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop to f6 and attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends, and now we have several choices, but 5...e6 is the Four Knights Sicilian. Our immediate plan is to continue developing quickly and actively with moves such as ...Bb4 and prepare the ...d5 break. Generally speaking, the ...d5 pawn break is a major goal for the Black pieces in the Sicilian.} 5...e6 6.Be3 {Many White players may already be out of book here and start playing "soft" moves such as Be3, which happens 25% of the time. Although it looks like a reasonable developing move, it does nothing to quell our aggressive central intentions of ...Bb4 and ...d5. So we'll go for ...Bb4, and the e4-pawn is already under attack.} 6...Bb4 7.f3 {The most common move in this position is f3, played just over 30% of the time. Another typical Sicilian move but also another mistake as White continues to fall behind in development. We'll execute our pawn break.} 7...d5 8.Nxc6 {White will most likely trade at this point. The idea is to remove our knight from c6 and push e5, closing the center. We'll take back, of course, and our center is strong.} 8...bxc6 9.e5 {White does push e5 now, but this is the wrong approach if we answer correctly. The most natural retreating square is d7, attacking the e5-pawn.} 9...Nd7 10.f4 {White protects it...} 10...O-O {But we will castle with a simple plan ahead and a comfortable lead in development. One idea is ...f6, breaking open the position to the benefit of our better developed pieces. The other one is ...c5, taking over the center. We could even implement both of these strong ideas soon.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Amateur Main Line (6.Be3?!) / Study 1: White Plays 7.Bd3, Nxc6 or f3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/34f13732-7c86-4f53-a808-071ebc0d161e/studies/b6f09bdf-0abd-472d-be7d-17be08a58e73/lines"]

1.e4 {The 4 Knights Sicilian is exactly what the name says: a Sicilian in which all 4 knights get developed before anything else. This can lead to positional and "boring" games, but usually makes for an interesting fight as there is a lot of control over the center early. In Chapter 1, we'll take a look at some of the most common moves at amateur level.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The first knight comes to f3. We'll develop ours to c6.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. These are the starting moves of the Open Sicilian, the most popular Sicilian variation. Almost 50% of games feature the Open Sicilian. That's more than 60 million games in the database! Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop to f6 and attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends, and now we have several choices, but 5...e6 is the Four Knights Sicilian. Our immediate plan is to continue developing quickly and actively with moves such as ...Bb4 and prepare the ...d5 break. Generally speaking, the ...d5 pawn break is a major goal for the Black pieces in the Sicilian.} 5...e6 6.Be3 {Many White players may already be out of book here and start playing "soft" moves such as Be3, which happens 25% of the time. Although it looks like a reasonable developing move, it does nothing to quell our aggressive central intentions of ...Bb4 and ...d5. So we'll go for ...Bb4, and the e4-pawn is already under attack.} 6...Bb4 7.Nxc6 {This trade is not a problem here. It happens 17% of the time. We'll recapture with the b-pawn, and ...d5 is coming anyway.} 7...bxc6 8.Bd3 {White still has to worry about the e4-pawn. Let's strike in the center as planned.} 8...d5 9.exd5 {White takes.} 9...cxd5 {We'll recapture with the c-pawn and connect our pawns. Castling is next. After that, we should look to place our rooks on the c- and b-files and put pressure on White's queenside. An easy position to play.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Amateur Main Line (6.Be3?!) / Study 1: White Plays 7.Bd3, Nxc6 or f3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/34f13732-7c86-4f53-a808-071ebc0d161e/studies/b6f09bdf-0abd-472d-be7d-17be08a58e73/lines"]

1.e4 {The 4 Knights Sicilian is exactly what the name says: a Sicilian in which all 4 knights get developed before anything else. This can lead to positional and "boring" games, but usually makes for an interesting fight as there is a lot of control over the center early. In Chapter 1, we'll take a look at some of the most common moves at amateur level.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The first knight comes to f3. We'll develop ours to c6.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. These are the starting moves of the Open Sicilian, the most popular Sicilian variation. Almost 50% of games feature the Open Sicilian. That's more than 60 million games in the database! Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop to f6 and attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends, and now we have several choices, but 5...e6 is the Four Knights Sicilian. Our immediate plan is to continue developing quickly and actively with moves such as ...Bb4 and prepare the ...d5 break. Generally speaking, the ...d5 pawn break is a major goal for the Black pieces in the Sicilian.} 5...e6 6.Be3 {Many White players may already be out of book here and start playing "soft" moves such as Be3, which happens 25% of the time. Although it looks like a reasonable developing move, it does nothing to quell our aggressive central intentions of ...Bb4 and ...d5. So we'll go for ...Bb4, and the e4-pawn is already under attack.} 6...Bb4 7.f3 {The most common move in this position is f3, played just over 30% of the time. Another typical Sicilian move but also another mistake as White continues to fall behind in development. We'll execute our pawn break.} 7...d5 8.Bb5 {A move that might look scary for a moment is 8.Bb5. White has defended the center with the pawn and now uses their light-squared bishop to apply pressure on our knight and pin it. But there's nothing to worry about.} 8...Qc7 {We can meet White's threat easily with a great position. The queen is active on the c-file and eyeing the kingside at the same time. We'll get castled next, and we also would like to play ...a6 to either force the trade on c6 or play ...b5.} *

