1.e4 {The 4 Knights Sicilian is exactly what the name says: a Sicilian in which all 4 knights get developed before anything else. This can lead to positional and "boring" games, but usually makes for an interesting fight as there is a lot of control over the center early. In Chapter 1, we'll take a look at some of the most common moves at amateur level.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The first knight comes to f3. We'll develop ours to c6.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. These are the starting moves of the Open Sicilian, the most popular Sicilian variation. Almost 50% of games feature the Open Sicilian. That's more than 60 million games in the database! Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop to f6 and attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends, and now we have several choices, but 5...e6 is the Four Knights Sicilian. Our immediate plan is to continue developing quickly and actively with moves such as ...Bb4 and prepare the ...d5 break. Generally speaking, the ...d5 pawn break is a major goal for the Black pieces in the Sicilian.} 5...e6 6.Be3 {Many White players may already be out of book here and start playing "soft" moves such as Be3, which happens 25% of the time. Although it looks like a reasonable developing move, it does nothing to quell our aggressive central intentions of ...Bb4 and ...d5. So we'll go for ...Bb4, and the e4-pawn is already under attack.} 6...Bb4 7.f3 {The most common move in this position is f3, played just over 30% of the time. Another typical Sicilian move but also another mistake as White continues to fall behind in development. We'll execute our pawn break.} 7...d5 8.exd5 {Taking on d5 is no good now.} 8...Nxd5 {We can recapture with the knight and White already has to be careful, as multiple pieces are under pressure. The best course of action for them is to trade on c6 and then play Bd2. But we've got a lead in activity even then.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Main Capture (6.Nxc6) / Study 1: White plays 6.Nxc6 and 8.Nxd5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/947171fb-8eb6-44e7-8c2e-f4fd8deb316e/studies/c691fbe1-cb9d-4b66-8bc3-611713fb60bc/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 2 will focus on the main line variations and lines most common at master level. In particular, the lines including White capturing our knight on move 6 with 6.Nxc6. It's often designed to push the e-pawn to e5 and try to create some trouble. Study 1 will take a look at 8.Nxd5.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Activating the knight. We'll do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures and there's tension between the knights. We'll develop the kingside knight first and attack the pawn on e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. And we'll play ...e6 with the plans we already saw in Chapter 1. Let's take a look at what we have here called "The Ultimate Main Line."} 5...e6 6.Nxc6 {It's White capturing on c6, which looks unorthodox because White trades off an active knight in the center for our knight on c6. It's ambitious, but leads to exciting play, and it's played 20% of the time, in almost 970,000 games at the amateur level. We'll recapture towards the center. If allowed, ...d5 would be great to play.} 6...bxc6 7.e5 {But White's whole idea is e5, which is actually the point of Nxc6. You can expect to see this move over 25% of the time, and likely close to 50% as you climb the rating ladder. White dominates the d6-square, preventing ...d5. If White doesn't play e5, our ...d5 idea works against basically any other move. Our knight was under attack, so we will jump to the center.} 7...Nd5 8.Nxd5 {8.Nxd5 happens 55% of the time and looks the most natural. However, we're actually happy to see this trade because it will give us a strong center and a comfortable position. We have ideas in the long run such as ...d6 to challenge the center or developing the light-squared bishop to a6 in the future. Our rook should use the b-file which has opened up.} 8...cxd5 9.Bd3 {White activates the bishop to d3 now and prepares castling. An immediate idea we could go for is targeting the e5-pawn. White defending it with f4 would slow down their development and allow ...Bc5, and otherwise the e5-pawn is simply a target.} 9...Qc7 10.Qe2 {White defends with the queen. But there is a downside to this as well. We can give a check on b4! The king has to move. Any alternative is worse than that.} 10...Bb4+ 11.c3 {The natural c3 is a blunder.} 11...Bxc3+ {We can take the pawn with check, and there's a fork on c3 if White recaptures.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Main Capture (6.Nxc6) / Study 1: White plays 6.Nxc6 and 8.Nxd5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/947171fb-8eb6-44e7-8c2e-f4fd8deb316e/studies/c691fbe1-cb9d-4b66-8bc3-611713fb60bc/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 2 will focus on the main line variations and lines most common at master level. In particular, the lines including White capturing our knight on move 6 with 6.Nxc6. It's often designed to push the e-pawn to e5 and try to create some trouble. Study 1 will take a look at 8.Nxd5.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Activating the knight. We'll do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures and there's tension between the knights. We'll develop the kingside knight first and attack the pawn on e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. And we'll play ...e6 with the plans we already saw in Chapter 1. Let's take a look at what we have here called "The Ultimate Main Line."} 5...e6 6.Nxc6 {It's White capturing on c6, which looks unorthodox because White trades off an active knight in the center for our knight on c6. It's ambitious, but leads to exciting play, and it's played 20% of the time, in almost 970,000 games at the amateur level. We'll recapture towards the center. If allowed, ...d5 would be great to play.} 6...bxc6 7.e5 {But White's whole idea is e5, which is actually the point of Nxc6. You can expect to see this move over 25% of the time, and likely close to 50% as you climb the rating ladder. White dominates the d6-square, preventing ...d5. If White doesn't play e5, our ...d5 idea works against basically any other move. Our knight was under attack, so we will jump to the center.} 7...Nd5 8.Nxd5 {8.Nxd5 happens 55% of the time and looks the most natural. However, we're actually happy to see this trade because it will give us a strong center and a comfortable position. We have ideas in the long run such as ...d6 to challenge the center or developing the light-squared bishop to a6 in the future. Our rook should use the b-file which has opened up.} 8...cxd5 9.Bd3 {White activates the bishop to d3 now and prepares castling. An immediate idea we could go for is targeting the e5-pawn. White defending it with f4 would slow down their development and allow ...Bc5, and otherwise the e5-pawn is simply a target.} 9...Qc7 10.Qe2 {White defends with the queen. But there is a downside to this as well. We can give a check on b4! The king has to move. Any alternative is worse than that.} 10...Bb4+ 11.Kf1 {The best move is Kf1. White is in no immediate danger but loses castling rights.} 11...Rb8 {We'll continue with ...Rb8, taking the semi-open file. Castling next is logical, and the bishop can retreat to c5 at some point. We can even undermine the e5-pawn with ...f6 after we castle, as it would give us the f-file for the rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Main Capture (6.Nxc6) / Study 1: White plays 6.Nxc6 and 8.Nxd5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/947171fb-8eb6-44e7-8c2e-f4fd8deb316e/studies/c691fbe1-cb9d-4b66-8bc3-611713fb60bc/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 2 will focus on the main line variations and lines most common at master level. In particular, the lines including White capturing our knight on move 6 with 6.Nxc6. It's often designed to push the e-pawn to e5 and try to create some trouble. Study 1 will take a look at 8.Nxd5.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Activating the knight. We'll do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures and there's tension between the knights. We'll develop the kingside knight first and attack the pawn on e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. And we'll play ...e6 with the plans we already saw in Chapter 1. Let's take a look at what we have here called "The Ultimate Main Line."} 5...e6 6.Nxc6 {It's White capturing on c6, which looks unorthodox because White trades off an active knight in the center for our knight on c6. It's ambitious, but leads to exciting play, and it's played 20% of the time, in almost 970,000 games at the amateur level. We'll recapture towards the center. If allowed, ...d5 would be great to play.} 6...bxc6 7.e5 {But White's whole idea is e5, which is actually the point of Nxc6. You can expect to see this move over 25% of the time, and likely close to 50% as you climb the rating ladder. White dominates the d6-square, preventing ...d5. If White doesn't play e5, our ...d5 idea works against basically any other move. Our knight was under attack, so we will jump to the center.} 7...Nd5 8.Nxd5 {8.Nxd5 happens 55% of the time and looks the most natural. However, we're actually happy to see this trade because it will give us a strong center and a comfortable position. We have ideas in the long run such as ...d6 to challenge the center or developing the light-squared bishop to a6 in the future. Our rook should use the b-file which has opened up.} 8...cxd5 9.Bd3 {White activates the bishop to d3 now and prepares castling. An immediate idea we could go for is targeting the e5-pawn. White defending it with f4 would slow down their development and allow ...Bc5, and otherwise the e5-pawn is simply a target.} 9...Qc7 10.Qe2 {White defends with the queen. But there is a downside to this as well. We can give a check on b4! The king has to move. Any alternative is worse than that.} 10...Bb4+ 11.Bd2 {Bd2 looks like it blocks the check properly, but White will have to move the king anyway. Because we will capture the bishop and keep an eye on the e5-pawn, so that White can't recapture with the queen.} 11...Bxd2+ 12.Kxd2 {The king takes instead.} 12...O-O {But now we have an easy game after castling. We'll open up the position with a pawn break like ...f6 or ...d6. White's king is out in the open and this is always better for us.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Main Capture (6.Nxc6) / Study 1: White plays 6.Nxc6 and 8.Nxd5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/947171fb-8eb6-44e7-8c2e-f4fd8deb316e/studies/c691fbe1-cb9d-4b66-8bc3-611713fb60bc/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 2 will focus on the main line variations and lines most common at master level. In particular, the lines including White capturing our knight on move 6 with 6.Nxc6. It's often designed to push the e-pawn to e5 and try to create some trouble. Study 1 will take a look at 8.Nxd5.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Activating the knight. We'll do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures and there's tension between the knights. We'll develop the kingside knight first and attack the pawn on e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. And we'll play ...e6 with the plans we already saw in Chapter 1. Let's take a look at what we have here called "The Ultimate Main Line."} 5...e6 6.Nxc6 {It's White capturing on c6, which looks unorthodox because White trades off an active knight in the center for our knight on c6. It's ambitious, but leads to exciting play, and it's played 20% of the time, in almost 970,000 games at the amateur level. We'll recapture towards the center. If allowed, ...d5 would be great to play.} 6...bxc6 7.Bd3 {White chooses 7.Bd3 nearly 30% of the time. White prefers the passive route and allow us to achieve a strong center, while they want to castle quickly. We'll play ...d5, which works every time. If White plays e5 now, simply retreat to d7 and you're fine. If they take in the center, it's better to recapture with the c-pawn.} 7...d5 8.O-O {White castles instead. We don't want to take in the center and activate their pieces. We got our space and it's time to continue developing now. We'll activate the bishop on e7.} 8...Be7 9.e5 {After we play ...d5, e5 is not much of a concern. We can always retreat to d7 and counterattack the pawn.} 9...Nd7 10.f4 {White players love to grab space like this with tempo. But we have a viable plan against f4 as well.} 10...a5 {And that's to play on the queenside and in the center, where we are the strongest. Our bishop on c8 is passive so trading it for White's bishop would be great. Apart from that, we can consider central expansions with ...d4 and ...c5, or placing the knight on c5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Main Capture (6.Nxc6) / Study 1: White plays 6.Nxc6 and 8.Nxd5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/947171fb-8eb6-44e7-8c2e-f4fd8deb316e/studies/c691fbe1-cb9d-4b66-8bc3-611713fb60bc/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 2 will focus on the main line variations and lines most common at master level. In particular, the lines including White capturing our knight on move 6 with 6.Nxc6. It's often designed to push the e-pawn to e5 and try to create some trouble. Study 1 will take a look at 8.Nxd5.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Activating the knight. We'll do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures and there's tension between the knights. We'll develop the kingside knight first and attack the pawn on e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. And we'll play ...e6 with the plans we already saw in Chapter 1. Let's take a look at what we have here called "The Ultimate Main Line."} 5...e6 6.Nxc6 {It's White capturing on c6, which looks unorthodox because White trades off an active knight in the center for our knight on c6. It's ambitious, but leads to exciting play, and it's played 20% of the time, in almost 970,000 games at the amateur level. We'll recapture towards the center. If allowed, ...d5 would be great to play.} 6...bxc6 7.Bg5 {Let's show another option that's played 25% of the time, and that's Bg5. White pins our knight and wants e5 now. Of course, it would be wonderful if this worked. But we've got ...d5 anyway! This move is essential to play.} 7...d5 8.e5 {It will lead to a sharp line after e5. White is attacking the pinned knight and we only have one response. That's to counterattack the bishop on g5.} 8...h6 9.Bh4 {A more sensible move from White is retreating to h4 and keeping the pin. This forces us to chase the bishop.} 9...g5 10.Bg3 {White retreats to g3.} 10...Nd7 {And we'll rotate back to d7 and attack the pawn on e5. We can continue to pile up on it with ...Bg7 and ...Qc7 if needed. All in all, we will probably win a pawn with no problems in the position.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Main Capture (6.Nxc6) / Study 1: White plays 6.Nxc6 and 8.Nxd5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/947171fb-8eb6-44e7-8c2e-f4fd8deb316e/studies/c691fbe1-cb9d-4b66-8bc3-611713fb60bc/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 2 will focus on the main line variations and lines most common at master level. In particular, the lines including White capturing our knight on move 6 with 6.Nxc6. It's often designed to push the e-pawn to e5 and try to create some trouble. Study 1 will take a look at 8.Nxd5.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Activating the knight. We'll do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures and there's tension between the knights. We'll develop the kingside knight first and attack the pawn on e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. And we'll play ...e6 with the plans we already saw in Chapter 1. Let's take a look at what we have here called "The Ultimate Main Line."} 5...e6 6.Nxc6 {It's White capturing on c6, which looks unorthodox because White trades off an active knight in the center for our knight on c6. It's ambitious, but leads to exciting play, and it's played 20% of the time, in almost 970,000 games at the amateur level. We'll recapture towards the center. If allowed, ...d5 would be great to play.} 6...bxc6 7.Bg5 {Let's show another option that's played 25% of the time, and that's Bg5. White pins our knight and wants e5 now. Of course, it would be wonderful if this worked. But we've got ...d5 anyway! This move is essential to play.} 7...d5 8.e5 {It will lead to a sharp line after e5. White is attacking the pinned knight and we only have one response. That's to counterattack the bishop on g5.} 8...h6 9.exf6 {White capturing on f6 actually loses the game. We'll take the bishop, of course.} 9...hxg5 10.fxg7 {If White doesn't want to lose a pawn, they will have to continue capturing.} 10...Bxg7 {We'll recapture and White's play has only helped us. The bishop on g7 is strong and the rook is coming to b8. We have the bishop pair and a lead in activity, not to mention White's non-existent center. Black is winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Main Capture (6.Nxc6) / Study 2: White plays 8.Ne4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/947171fb-8eb6-44e7-8c2e-f4fd8deb316e/studies/e1876cbb-f758-4a53-82e6-410393875244/lines"]

1.e4 {After looking at 8.Nxc6, which could be considered an inaccuracy, let's focus on the best move for White on move 8, which is 8.Ne4. White logically supports their control of the center and aims for the d6-square.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Developing the knight to f3. We'll activate a knight as well.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. And we'll prepare ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Nxc6 {White will fight against it by capturing first. We'll recapture.} 6...bxc6 7.e5 {And now e5 claims further control of the d6-square. We'll centralize the knight which was under attack.} 7...Nd5 8.Ne4 {In this study, White doesn't trade but rather plays Ne4. This move is played 33% of the time, in more than 100,000 games. White wants to completely dominate the d6-square and disallow any ...d6 advancements, with ideas of c4 or Nd6+. We'll fianchetto, with the idea of ...c5 soon.} 8...Bb7 9.c4 {The most common and the most logical move is c4, chasing our knight. We'll jump to b4 rather than b6, and we have a trick up our sleeve if White is persistent. One option for White here is the immediate Nd6+. We would neutralize that by taking on d6 and going for ...Qa5 and ...Qe5 ideas.} 9...Nb4 10.a3 {And they can be persistent with a3. It might be too passive to retreat to a6 now. Let's slide the queen to a5 instead and pin the a-pawn to the rook. The queen is also attacking the e5-pawn.} 10...Qa5 11.Bd2 {White pins our knight back. So we have to capture in the center, counterattacking White's knight.} 11...Qxe5 12.axb4 {This trade is fine. We'll get a lot of activity. We'll capture with check.} 12...Qxe4+ 13.Be2 {The bishop blocking is a big mistake.} 13...c5 {The main point is ...c5, opening up our bishop on b7. We want to open up our dark-squared bishop as well. White's space advantage is gone and now we are the ones in the driver's seat. White's pieces are passive and there are suddenly a lot of problems for them to deal with.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Main Capture (6.Nxc6) / Study 2: White plays 8.Ne4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/947171fb-8eb6-44e7-8c2e-f4fd8deb316e/studies/e1876cbb-f758-4a53-82e6-410393875244/lines"]

1.e4 {After looking at 8.Nxc6, which could be considered an inaccuracy, let's focus on the best move for White on move 8, which is 8.Ne4. White logically supports their control of the center and aims for the d6-square.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Developing the knight to f3. We'll activate a knight as well.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. And we'll prepare ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Nxc6 {White will fight against it by capturing first. We'll recapture.} 6...bxc6 7.e5 {And now e5 claims further control of the d6-square. We'll centralize the knight which was under attack.} 7...Nd5 8.Ne4 {In this study, White doesn't trade but rather plays Ne4. This move is played 33% of the time, in more than 100,000 games. White wants to completely dominate the d6-square and disallow any ...d6 advancements, with ideas of c4 or Nd6+. We'll fianchetto, with the idea of ...c5 soon.} 8...Bb7 9.c4 {The most common and the most logical move is c4, chasing our knight. We'll jump to b4 rather than b6, and we have a trick up our sleeve if White is persistent. One option for White here is the immediate Nd6+. We would neutralize that by taking on d6 and going for ...Qa5 and ...Qe5 ideas.} 9...Nb4 10.a3 {And they can be persistent with a3. It might be too passive to retreat to a6 now. Let's slide the queen to a5 instead and pin the a-pawn to the rook. The queen is also attacking the e5-pawn.} 10...Qa5 11.Bd2 {White pins our knight back. So we have to capture in the center, counterattacking White's knight.} 11...Qxe5 12.f3 {It's probably slightly better for White to avoid the trade of knights. Our knight has to come back to a6 while theirs stays active in the center.} 12...Na6 13.Bc3 {And they get to improve with Bc3.} 13...Qc7 {Let's retreat to c7 with other ideas in mind. Now that the e5-pawn is gone, we can look to play ...d5 and ...c5 or ...Nc5 to offer a knight trade. We will also develop with ...Be7. We have a great position and more central pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Main Capture (6.Nxc6) / Study 2: White plays 8.Ne4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/947171fb-8eb6-44e7-8c2e-f4fd8deb316e/studies/e1876cbb-f758-4a53-82e6-410393875244/lines"]

1.e4 {After looking at 8.Nxc6, which could be considered an inaccuracy, let's focus on the best move for White on move 8, which is 8.Ne4. White logically supports their control of the center and aims for the d6-square.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Developing the knight to f3. We'll activate a knight as well.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. And we'll prepare ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Nxc6 {White will fight against it by capturing first. We'll recapture.} 6...bxc6 7.e5 {And now e5 claims further control of the d6-square. We'll centralize the knight which was under attack.} 7...Nd5 8.Ne4 {In this study, White doesn't trade but rather plays Ne4. This move is played 33% of the time, in more than 100,000 games. White wants to completely dominate the d6-square and disallow any ...d6 advancements, with ideas of c4 or Nd6+. We'll fianchetto, with the idea of ...c5 soon.} 8...Bb7 9.c4 {The most common and the most logical move is c4, chasing our knight. We'll jump to b4 rather than b6, and we have a trick up our sleeve if White is persistent. One option for White here is the immediate Nd6+. We would neutralize that by taking on d6 and going for ...Qa5 and ...Qe5 ideas.} 9...Nb4 10.c5 {The best move is probably c5, although it's not the most popular one. White completely shuts down our bishop on b7 and tries to suffocate our position. It's crucial to target weaknesses, so I suggest ...Qb8 with an attack on the e5-pawn.} 10...Qb8 11.f4 {The most sensible move is f4. White is all in with the pawns. We have to find a way to maneuver, though. A good option is to trade the light-squared bishops, since ours wasn't doing anything.} 11...Ba6 12.Bxa6 {White decides to take. We'll recapture.} 12...Nxa6 13.Nd6+ {When White has pawns on both c5 and e5, you should expect a check on d6. We'll take, of course.} 13...Bxd6 14.cxd6 {The c-pawn recaptures and White does have a strong pawn chain deep in our camp. But we have our important open b-file.} 14...Qb4+ {Which we can use to improve the queen here. Our knight is coming to e4 via c5 and we'll play ...f5 to expand. White's pawns won't be a huge problem.} *

