[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/947171fb-8eb6-44e7-8c2e-f4fd8deb316e/studies/e1876cbb-f758-4a53-82e6-410393875244/lines"]

1.e4 {After looking at 8.Nxc6, which could be considered an inaccuracy, let's focus on the best move for White on move 8, which is 8.Ne4. White logically supports their control of the center and aims for the d6-square.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Developing the knight to f3. We'll activate a knight as well.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. And we'll prepare ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Nxc6 {White will fight against it by capturing first. We'll recapture.} 6...bxc6 7.e5 {And now e5 claims further control of the d6-square. We'll centralize the knight which was under attack.} 7...Nd5 8.Ne4 {In this study, White doesn't trade but rather plays Ne4. This move is played 33% of the time, in more than 100,000 games. White wants to completely dominate the d6-square and disallow any ...d6 advancements, with ideas of c4 or Nd6+. We'll fianchetto, with the idea of ...c5 soon.} 8...Bb7 9.c4 {The most common and the most logical move is c4, chasing our knight. We'll jump to b4 rather than b6, and we have a trick up our sleeve if White is persistent. One option for White here is the immediate Nd6+. We would neutralize that by taking on d6 and going for ...Qa5 and ...Qe5 ideas.} 9...Nb4 10.c5 {The best move is probably c5, although it's not the most popular one. White completely shuts down our bishop on b7 and tries to suffocate our position. It's crucial to target weaknesses, so I suggest ...Qb8 with an attack on the e5-pawn.} 10...Qb8 11.f4 {The most sensible move is f4. White is all in with the pawns. We have to find a way to maneuver, though. A good option is to trade the light-squared bishops, since ours wasn't doing anything.} 11...Ba6 12.Bxa6 {White decides to take. We'll recapture.} 12...Nxa6 13.Qd4 {Instead of giving the check on d6, White can centralize the queen on d4. The queen is very active on that square and could pose problems. That's why we should trade. And we can force the trade here.} 13...Qb4+ 14.Qxb4 {The queen recaptures.} 14...Nxb4 {We'll take back with the knight, and it's coming to d5. We will be able to target White's defenses from there. The bishop will be fine on e7 and we'll castle, also planning for the a-rook to come to b8.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Main Capture (6.Nxc6) / Study 2: White plays 8.Ne4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/947171fb-8eb6-44e7-8c2e-f4fd8deb316e/studies/e1876cbb-f758-4a53-82e6-410393875244/lines"]

1.e4 {After looking at 8.Nxc6, which could be considered an inaccuracy, let's focus on the best move for White on move 8, which is 8.Ne4. White logically supports their control of the center and aims for the d6-square.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Developing the knight to f3. We'll activate a knight as well.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. And we'll prepare ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Nxc6 {White will fight against it by capturing first. We'll recapture.} 6...bxc6 7.e5 {And now e5 claims further control of the d6-square. We'll centralize the knight which was under attack.} 7...Nd5 8.Ne4 {In this study, White doesn't trade but rather plays Ne4. This move is played 33% of the time, in more than 100,000 games. White wants to completely dominate the d6-square and disallow any ...d6 advancements, with ideas of c4 or Nd6+. We'll fianchetto, with the idea of ...c5 soon.} 8...Bb7 9.c4 {The most common and the most logical move is c4, chasing our knight. We'll jump to b4 rather than b6, and we have a trick up our sleeve if White is persistent. One option for White here is the immediate Nd6+. We would neutralize that by taking on d6 and going for ...Qa5 and ...Qe5 ideas.} 9...Nb4 10.c5 {The best move is probably c5, although it's not the most popular one. White completely shuts down our bishop on b7 and tries to suffocate our position. It's crucial to target weaknesses, so I suggest ...Qb8 with an attack on the e5-pawn.} 10...Qb8 11.f4 {The most sensible move is f4. White is all in with the pawns. We have to find a way to maneuver, though. A good option is to trade the light-squared bishops, since ours wasn't doing anything.} 11...Ba6 12.a3 {If White goes for a3, trying to kick away our bishop's defender, we don't really have a choice. We'll take on f1.} 12...Bxf1 13.Rxf1 {The rook recaptures.} 13...Nd5 {And now we can rotate back to d5 where our knight is safe. A good plan to continue is ...f5, attacking the knight and blocking the position. We wouldn't mind White trading with en passant in that scenario.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Main Capture (6.Nxc6) / Study 2: White plays 8.Ne4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/947171fb-8eb6-44e7-8c2e-f4fd8deb316e/studies/e1876cbb-f758-4a53-82e6-410393875244/lines"]

1.e4 {After looking at 8.Nxc6, which could be considered an inaccuracy, let's focus on the best move for White on move 8, which is 8.Ne4. White logically supports their control of the center and aims for the d6-square.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Developing the knight to f3. We'll activate a knight as well.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. And we'll prepare ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Nxc6 {White will fight against it by capturing first. We'll recapture.} 6...bxc6 7.e5 {And now e5 claims further control of the d6-square. We'll centralize the knight which was under attack.} 7...Nd5 8.Ne4 {In this study, White doesn't trade but rather plays Ne4. This move is played 33% of the time, in more than 100,000 games. White wants to completely dominate the d6-square and disallow any ...d6 advancements, with ideas of c4 or Nd6+. We'll fianchetto, with the idea of ...c5 soon.} 8...Bb7 9.c4 {The most common and the most logical move is c4, chasing our knight. We'll jump to b4 rather than b6, and we have a trick up our sleeve if White is persistent. One option for White here is the immediate Nd6+. We would neutralize that by taking on d6 and going for ...Qa5 and ...Qe5 ideas.} 9...Nb4 10.c5 {The best move is probably c5, although it's not the most popular one. White completely shuts down our bishop on b7 and tries to suffocate our position. It's crucial to target weaknesses, so I suggest ...Qb8 with an attack on the e5-pawn.} 10...Qb8 11.Nd6+ {One option for White is to block with the knight and give a check. We'll capture.} 11...Bxd6 12.exd6 {The d-pawn recaptures. Those pawns on d6 and c5 do look scary, but we can actually develop around them. We'll improve our bishop to a6 first and offer a trade, which is one benefit of having our knight on b4.} 12...Ba6 13.Bxa6 {White takes. We'll recapture with the knight. White will definitely try to utilize their space advantage.} 13...Nxa6 14.Qd4 {One such possibility is Qd4 with an attack on g7. We don't want to castle and allow Bd2, with White basically trapping our pieces. Let's force a queen trade instead. This will weaken White's pawns and give us some breathing room.} 14...Qb4+ 15.Qxb4 {White takes.} 15...Nxb4 {We'll recapture with the knight which now has an outpost on d5, since White has committed the pawn to c5. Our pieces will get out via ...f6 and ...Kf7 and we will play against White's extended pawns in the long run.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Main Capture (6.Nxc6) / Study 2: White plays 8.Ne4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/947171fb-8eb6-44e7-8c2e-f4fd8deb316e/studies/e1876cbb-f758-4a53-82e6-410393875244/lines"]

1.e4 {After looking at 8.Nxc6, which could be considered an inaccuracy, let's focus on the best move for White on move 8, which is 8.Ne4. White logically supports their control of the center and aims for the d6-square.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Developing the knight to f3. We'll activate a knight as well.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. And we'll prepare ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Nxc6 {White will fight against it by capturing first. We'll recapture.} 6...bxc6 7.e5 {And now e5 claims further control of the d6-square. We'll centralize the knight which was under attack.} 7...Nd5 8.Ne4 {In this study, White doesn't trade but rather plays Ne4. This move is played 33% of the time, in more than 100,000 games. White wants to completely dominate the d6-square and disallow any ...d6 advancements, with ideas of c4 or Nd6+. We'll fianchetto, with the idea of ...c5 soon.} 8...Bb7 9.Be2 {White can choose the calmer route and develop the bishop to e2, preparing castling. This gives us time to open up our light-squared bishop.} 9...c5 10.O-O {White castles. And we'll continue improving and attack the pawn on e5. It actually isn't so straightforward to defend it.} 10...Qc7 11.Nd6+ {The best move is to give a check on d6. We have to take.} 11...Bxd6 12.exd6 {The d-pawn recaptures. White's idea is c4 if we take the pawn on d6, but we don't have to capture it. We can place the queen on c6 and create a battery on the light squares, aiming towards the king.} 12...Qc6 13.f3 {White can choose to defend the king with the pawn. It does look unnatural compared to Bf3, but White wants c4 anyway. So let's play it ourselves and prevent them from playing it.} 13...c4 14.Qd4 {White does get to centralize the queen, but the attack on the g7-pawn is easily stopped. We'll castle, now actually threatening to win the d6-pawn.} 14...O-O 15.Bxc4 {We did give up c4 in the process. But we got rid of the annoying pawn, which was the goal all along. Looking at the position now, we could continue to play on the queenside with ...Rfc8 and offer a queen trade. White's queen on d4 is their most active piece.} 15...Qxd6 16.b3 {White goes for b3 now with the goal of Bb2. Let's occupy the c-file.} 16...Rfc8 17.Bb2 {White can threaten mate in one.} 17...Qb6 {But we can stop it by pinning their queen to the king. We'll get rid of the queens if White doesn't. After that, we could consider playing for ...d5.} *

