1.e4 {After looking at 8.Nxc6, which could be considered an inaccuracy, let's focus on the best move for White on move 8, which is 8.Ne4. White logically supports their control of the center and aims for the d6-square.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Developing the knight to f3. We'll activate a knight as well.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. And we'll prepare ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Nxc6 {White will fight against it by capturing first. We'll recapture.} 6...bxc6 7.e5 {And now e5 claims further control of the d6-square. We'll centralize the knight which was under attack.} 7...Nd5 8.Ne4 {In this study, White doesn't trade but rather plays Ne4. This move is played 33% of the time, in more than 100,000 games. White wants to completely dominate the d6-square and disallow any ...d6 advancements, with ideas of c4 or Nd6+. We'll fianchetto, with the idea of ...c5 soon.} 8...Bb7 9.Be2 {White can choose the calmer route and develop the bishop to e2, preparing castling. This gives us time to open up our light-squared bishop.} 9...c5 10.O-O {White castles. And we'll continue improving and attack the pawn on e5. It actually isn't so straightforward to defend it.} 10...Qc7 11.Nd6+ {The best move is to give a check on d6. We have to take.} 11...Bxd6 12.exd6 {The d-pawn recaptures. White's idea is c4 if we take the pawn on d6, but we don't have to capture it. We can place the queen on c6 and create a battery on the light squares, aiming towards the king.} 12...Qc6 13.Bf3 {A sensible defensive effort is Bf3, blocking the diagonal and threatening c4. We'll prevent it by playing ...c4 ourselves. It does come with a downside, though.} 13...c4 14.Qd4 {It gives White the d4-square. We'll castle and defend the kingside. Now there is an actual threat on the d6-pawn.} 14...O-O 15.Bf4 {White defends it with the bishop. Our queen isn't playing much of a role apart from attacking the pawn.} 15...Qb6 {So let's offer a trade of queens. This will further weaken the d6-pawn and we will be able to play ...f6 and ...e5 to win it. We should be up a pawn eventually.} *

