[Event "Chapter 2: The Main Capture (6.Nxc6) / Study 2: White plays 8.Ne4 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/947171fb-8eb6-44e7-8c2e-f4fd8deb316e/studies/e1876cbb-f758-4a53-82e6-410393875244/lines"]

1.e4 {After looking at 8.Nxc6, which could be considered an inaccuracy, let's focus on the best move for White on move 8, which is 8.Ne4. White logically supports their control of the center and aims for the d6-square.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Developing the knight to f3. We'll activate a knight as well.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. And we'll prepare ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Nxc6 {White will fight against it by capturing first. We'll recapture.} 6...bxc6 7.e5 {And now e5 claims further control of the d6-square. We'll centralize the knight which was under attack.} 7...Nd5 8.Ne4 {In this study, White doesn't trade but rather plays Ne4. This move is played 33% of the time, in more than 100,000 games. White wants to completely dominate the d6-square and disallow any ...d6 advancements, with ideas of c4 or Nd6+. We'll fianchetto, with the idea of ...c5 soon.} 8...Bb7 9.Be2 {White can choose the calmer route and develop the bishop to e2, preparing castling. This gives us time to open up our light-squared bishop.} 9...c5 10.O-O {White castles. And we'll continue improving and attack the pawn on e5. It actually isn't so straightforward to defend it.} 10...Qc7 11.c4 {You can expect c4 at any point. However, when our bishop is on b7 and we've already played ...c5, it's different. We can sacrifice the knight on e3!} 11...Ne3 12.Bxe3 {White has to take because it's a fork. And we'll take the knight on e4. The e5-pawn is still under attack.} 12...Bxe4 13.f4 {White defends, but we've successfully fought against their setup.} 13...Be7 {There is no knight on e4 and we've kept the active bishops. Now we can begin to undermine their structure with ...d6 and/or ...f6.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The 6.Bg5 Pin / Study 1: Four Knights with 6.Bg5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/c3554f74-942b-4a18-b83c-41707d0fa1dd/studies/5baca9a5-035a-4723-9476-fab3b6d271e0/lines"]

1.e4 {The third chapter will show a specific line by White which is characterized by the move 6.Bg5. It looks like a completely logical move: White pins our knight and threatens to push e5 at some point. However, this is another common inaccuracy which we can exploit down the line.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Developing the knight. We'll do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White expands in the center. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures. Let's develop a knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White protects the pawn. We'll go for ...e6.} 5...e6 6.Bg5 {White goes for 6.Bg5 15% of the time, as seen in 720,000 games. As mentioned, the idea is to trade on c6 and push e5, attacking our pinned knight. However, that bishop might become exposed to various ...Qa5 ideas, as you will see. We'll play ...Bb4 immediately and set a trap, and our queen is coming to a5.} 6...Bb4 7.Nxc6 {White can capture on c6. We'll recapture with the b-pawn.} 7...bxc6 8.e5 {Going for e5 now is a mistake. It seems like White is winning a piece, but we have ...Qa5, counter pinning the e5-pawn! The knight on c3 is also under attack twice.} 8...Qa5 9.exf6 {If White captures on f6, the bishop on g5 is hanging. So we'll take it.} 9...Qxg5 10.fxg7 {White captures on g7 now.} 10...Qxg7 {And we'll recapture with the queen, keeping it active on the long diagonal. The knight on c3 is still vulnerable and we are looking for ...d5. Black is much better. Perhaps 6.Bg5 is not the best option for White.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The 6.Bg5 Pin / Study 1: Four Knights with 6.Bg5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/c3554f74-942b-4a18-b83c-41707d0fa1dd/studies/5baca9a5-035a-4723-9476-fab3b6d271e0/lines"]

1.e4 {The third chapter will show a specific line by White which is characterized by the move 6.Bg5. It looks like a completely logical move: White pins our knight and threatens to push e5 at some point. However, this is another common inaccuracy which we can exploit down the line.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Developing the knight. We'll do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White expands in the center. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures. Let's develop a knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White protects the pawn. We'll go for ...e6.} 5...e6 6.Bg5 {White goes for 6.Bg5 15% of the time, as seen in 720,000 games. As mentioned, the idea is to trade on c6 and push e5, attacking our pinned knight. However, that bishop might become exposed to various ...Qa5 ideas, as you will see. We'll play ...Bb4 immediately and set a trap, and our queen is coming to a5.} 6...Bb4 7.Nxc6 {White can capture on c6. We'll recapture with the b-pawn.} 7...bxc6 8.e5 {Going for e5 now is a mistake. It seems like White is winning a piece, but we have ...Qa5, counter pinning the e5-pawn! The knight on c3 is also under attack twice.} 8...Qa5 9.Qd4 {White can try to defend with Qd4. But we're not done applying pressure. We can play ...Ne4, adding a third attacker to the knight and attacking the bishop on g5 as well. If White takes on e4 with the queen, we'll capture on c3.} 9...Ne4 10.Bd2 {White can try to retreat to d2 instead. But we'll capture, and the pawn on e5 is under attack as well, so White can't really recapture with the queen.} 10...Nxd2 11.Kxd2 {Taking with the king looks sad.} 11...Rb8 {We'll place the rook on the b-file and we have all the activity in the world. White's pieces are tied down to the knight on c3 and Black is winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Main Line Cobra (6.Ndb5) / Study 1: White plays 7.Nd6+ / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/a5585b11-3230-4007-80fb-9b0102a2c767/studies/10806b6b-67bf-4f0f-bbf7-6c0b27218d20/lines"]

1.e4 {Having considered some of the most popular sixth moves at the amateur level, let's focus on the most common move at master level, 6.Nbd5, which is the Cobra variation. Instead of trading the knights or developing the dark-squared bishops, White takes control of the d6-square immediately with the knight. We will have to implement a very unconventional approach against this.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates a knight. Let's do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {Going for central expansion. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop both knights now.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends the pawn. Preparing ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Ndb5 {The Cobra variation. White wants to get to the d6-square quickly, and we can't play ...d5 to stop it (there are Nc7+ tactics eventually). At the amateur level, this move only happens 8% of the time, which is still almost 400,000 games. In fact, let's develop the dark-squared bishop!} 6...Bc5 7.Nd6+ {In Study 1, we will analyze White checking us on d6, which happens more than 70% of the time. We played ...Bc5 so that we can capture on d6 once the knight arrives there, right? Nope! We do not want to trade our dark-squared bishop but rather claim that our king will be safe on e7. In fact, White's knight is the one under attack.} 7...Ke7 8.Nxc8+ {8.Nxc8+ doesn't make a whole lot of sense if White wanted activity, but it's the most common move in this position, played 35% of the time. We'll recapture with the rook. White might think they are still in the driver's seat here because our king is on e7, but the truth is that they are the ones who have to be accurate not to lose the game.} 8...Rxc8 9.Bd3 {A safe move is to develop the bishop to d3 and protect the pawn on e4. Let's develop the queen to c7, which is an active square for it to target the kingside. We are happy to play on the dark squares.} 9...Qc7 10.O-O {If White castles, we can launch an attack immediately. Let's see if White can defend. Our knight is looking at the g4-square now, from where we would threaten checkmate on h2 together with the queen. Our bishop is strong on c5 and attacking the f2-pawn.} 10...h5 11.Bg5 {White prevents ...Ng4 with a pin. Which we will get rid of immediately. Now ...Ng4 is a threat again.} 11...Kf8 12.Bxf6 {White has to capture.} 12...gxf6 {We'll take back and we have ideas of pushing the h-pawn now, together with the aggressive ...Ne5. The g-file has also opened up. Our king is very safe now and we control the dark squares. Meanwhile, White's king is still in danger.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Main Line Cobra (6.Ndb5) / Study 1: White plays 7.Nd6+ / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/a5585b11-3230-4007-80fb-9b0102a2c767/studies/10806b6b-67bf-4f0f-bbf7-6c0b27218d20/lines"]

1.e4 {Having considered some of the most popular sixth moves at the amateur level, let's focus on the most common move at master level, 6.Nbd5, which is the Cobra variation. Instead of trading the knights or developing the dark-squared bishops, White takes control of the d6-square immediately with the knight. We will have to implement a very unconventional approach against this.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates a knight. Let's do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {Going for central expansion. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop both knights now.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends the pawn. Preparing ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Ndb5 {The Cobra variation. White wants to get to the d6-square quickly, and we can't play ...d5 to stop it (there are Nc7+ tactics eventually). At the amateur level, this move only happens 8% of the time, which is still almost 400,000 games. In fact, let's develop the dark-squared bishop!} 6...Bc5 7.Nd6+ {In Study 1, we will analyze White checking us on d6, which happens more than 70% of the time. We played ...Bc5 so that we can capture on d6 once the knight arrives there, right? Nope! We do not want to trade our dark-squared bishop but rather claim that our king will be safe on e7. In fact, White's knight is the one under attack.} 7...Ke7 8.Nxc8+ {8.Nxc8+ doesn't make a whole lot of sense if White wanted activity, but it's the most common move in this position, played 35% of the time. We'll recapture with the rook. White might think they are still in the driver's seat here because our king is on e7, but the truth is that they are the ones who have to be accurate not to lose the game.} 8...Rxc8 9.Bd3 {A safe move is to develop the bishop to d3 and protect the pawn on e4. Let's develop the queen to c7, which is an active square for it to target the kingside. We are happy to play on the dark squares.} 9...Qc7 10.O-O {If White castles, we can launch an attack immediately. Let's see if White can defend. Our knight is looking at the g4-square now, from where we would threaten checkmate on h2 together with the queen. Our bishop is strong on c5 and attacking the f2-pawn.} 10...h5 11.h3 {The move h3 is a huge blunder. It doesn't stop our attack at all. We are still jumping to g4 with the same threat. Of course, g3 is not possible because of ...Qxg3+.} 11...Ng4 12.hxg4 {White captures on g4. We'll recapture and the h-file is now open. Here's one fun sample line on how to convert this crushing position.} 12...hxg4 13.Re1 {White opens up a square for the king. We'll check on h2.} 13...Qh2+ 14.Kf1 {White slides to f1. Let's centralize the knight and take control of the e2-square. Now ...Qh1# is the threat.} 14...Nd4 15.Ne2 {White retreats to e2, so that the knight can block on g1.} 15...Nf3 {But we can jump to f3 with the same threat! If White takes on f3, there's checkmate on f2.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Main Line Cobra (6.Ndb5) / Study 1: White plays 7.Nd6+ / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/a5585b11-3230-4007-80fb-9b0102a2c767/studies/10806b6b-67bf-4f0f-bbf7-6c0b27218d20/lines"]

1.e4 {Having considered some of the most popular sixth moves at the amateur level, let's focus on the most common move at master level, 6.Nbd5, which is the Cobra variation. Instead of trading the knights or developing the dark-squared bishops, White takes control of the d6-square immediately with the knight. We will have to implement a very unconventional approach against this.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates a knight. Let's do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {Going for central expansion. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop both knights now.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends the pawn. Preparing ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Ndb5 {The Cobra variation. White wants to get to the d6-square quickly, and we can't play ...d5 to stop it (there are Nc7+ tactics eventually). At the amateur level, this move only happens 8% of the time, which is still almost 400,000 games. In fact, let's develop the dark-squared bishop!} 6...Bc5 7.Nd6+ {In Study 1, we will analyze White checking us on d6, which happens more than 70% of the time. We played ...Bc5 so that we can capture on d6 once the knight arrives there, right? Nope! We do not want to trade our dark-squared bishop but rather claim that our king will be safe on e7. In fact, White's knight is the one under attack.} 7...Ke7 8.Nxc8+ {8.Nxc8+ doesn't make a whole lot of sense if White wanted activity, but it's the most common move in this position, played 35% of the time. We'll recapture with the rook. White might think they are still in the driver's seat here because our king is on e7, but the truth is that they are the ones who have to be accurate not to lose the game.} 8...Rxc8 9.Bg5 {For example, 9.Bg5 is played almost 30% of the time in this position, and it loses the game on the spot. Don't forget ...Qb6 as a strong weapon in some instances. We are forking the pawn on f2 and the pawn on b2. Believe it or not, it's not easy for White to defend against this.} 9...Qb6 10.Qd2 {They can try to defend with the queen. But we will sacrifice the bishop on f2!} 10...Bxf2+ 11.Qxf2 {The point of this sacrifice was to deflect the queen from guarding the knight on c3.} 11...Qxb2 {And now there's an easy fork on b2. We will regain the piece with a winning position.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Main Line Cobra (6.Ndb5) / Study 1: White plays 7.Nd6+ / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/a5585b11-3230-4007-80fb-9b0102a2c767/studies/10806b6b-67bf-4f0f-bbf7-6c0b27218d20/lines"]

1.e4 {Having considered some of the most popular sixth moves at the amateur level, let's focus on the most common move at master level, 6.Nbd5, which is the Cobra variation. Instead of trading the knights or developing the dark-squared bishops, White takes control of the d6-square immediately with the knight. We will have to implement a very unconventional approach against this.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates a knight. Let's do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {Going for central expansion. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop both knights now.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends the pawn. Preparing ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Ndb5 {The Cobra variation. White wants to get to the d6-square quickly, and we can't play ...d5 to stop it (there are Nc7+ tactics eventually). At the amateur level, this move only happens 8% of the time, which is still almost 400,000 games. In fact, let's develop the dark-squared bishop!} 6...Bc5 7.Nd6+ {In Study 1, we will analyze White checking us on d6, which happens more than 70% of the time. We played ...Bc5 so that we can capture on d6 once the knight arrives there, right? Nope! We do not want to trade our dark-squared bishop but rather claim that our king will be safe on e7. In fact, White's knight is the one under attack.} 7...Ke7 8.Bf4 {The best move for White is 8.Bf4, and it's played 28% of the time. It's best for White to hold on to the d6-square a while longer. We'll chase the pieces away, but at the cost of the d5-square. White has to react now as both the bishop and the knight are under pressure.} 8...e5 9.Nxc8+ {We don't mind White trading on c8, as that knight was annoying. We'll take with the rook.} 9...Rxc8 10.Bg5 {Pinning our knight makes sense now, since White is threatening Nd5+. We have to stop that.} 10...Bb4 {And we will if we pin the knight on c3. This is very important. Next, we will capture on c3 and break White's pin with either ...h6 or the maneuver consisting of ...Qc7, ...Nd8 and ...Ne6.} *

