1.e4 {Having considered some of the most popular sixth moves at the amateur level, let's focus on the most common move at master level, 6.Nbd5, which is the Cobra variation. Instead of trading the knights or developing the dark-squared bishops, White takes control of the d6-square immediately with the knight. We will have to implement a very unconventional approach against this.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates a knight. Let's do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {Going for central expansion. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop both knights now.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends the pawn. Preparing ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Ndb5 {The Cobra variation. White wants to get to the d6-square quickly, and we can't play ...d5 to stop it (there are Nc7+ tactics eventually). At the amateur level, this move only happens 8% of the time, which is still almost 400,000 games. In fact, let's develop the dark-squared bishop!} 6...Bc5 7.Nd6+ {In Study 1, we will analyze White checking us on d6, which happens more than 70% of the time. We played ...Bc5 so that we can capture on d6 once the knight arrives there, right? Nope! We do not want to trade our dark-squared bishop but rather claim that our king will be safe on e7. In fact, White's knight is the one under attack.} 7...Ke7 8.Bf4 {The best move for White is 8.Bf4, and it's played 28% of the time. It's best for White to hold on to the d6-square a while longer. We'll chase the pieces away, but at the cost of the d5-square. White has to react now as both the bishop and the knight are under pressure.} 8...e5 9.Nf5+ {A move that I'd say makes more sense than trading on c8 is Nf5+. White saves the knight but keeps it active. We'll retreat to the safe f8-square, and the bishop on f4 is still under attack.} 9...Kf8 10.Bg5 {A more dangerous alternative than Be3 is White creating this pin and fighting for control of the vulnerable d5-square. We will play ...d6 and allow their knight to come to d5 if that's what they want, opening the e6-square for our bishop with combined ideas of ...h6 and ...g6. This also sets a trap.} 10...d6 11.Nd5 {The natural Nd5 loses the game. We have ...Qa5+ with a check! Suddenly, the pin is gone and the e4-pawn is under attack.} 11...Qa5+ 12.c3 {White will probably block with the pawn.} 12...Nxe4 {But we are completely winning after ...Nxe4. All of White's pressure is gone, their knight on f5 is under attack, their pawn on f2 is under attack, their bishop on g5 is under attack and the c3-pawn is vulnerable. A lot of problems.} *

