1.e4 {Having considered some of the most popular sixth moves at the amateur level, let's focus on the most common move at master level, 6.Nbd5, which is the Cobra variation. Instead of trading the knights or developing the dark-squared bishops, White takes control of the d6-square immediately with the knight. We will have to implement a very unconventional approach against this.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates a knight. Let's do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {Going for central expansion. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop both knights now.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends the pawn. Preparing ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Ndb5 {The Cobra variation. White wants to get to the d6-square quickly, and we can't play ...d5 to stop it (there are Nc7+ tactics eventually). At the amateur level, this move only happens 8% of the time, which is still almost 400,000 games. In fact, let's develop the dark-squared bishop!} 6...Bc5 7.Nd6+ {In Study 1, we will analyze White checking us on d6, which happens more than 70% of the time. We played ...Bc5 so that we can capture on d6 once the knight arrives there, right? Nope! We do not want to trade our dark-squared bishop but rather claim that our king will be safe on e7. In fact, White's knight is the one under attack.} 7...Ke7 8.Bf4 {The best move for White is 8.Bf4, and it's played 28% of the time. It's best for White to hold on to the d6-square a while longer. We'll chase the pieces away, but at the cost of the d5-square. White has to react now as both the bishop and the knight are under pressure.} 8...e5 9.Nf5+ {A move that I'd say makes more sense than trading on c8 is Nf5+. White saves the knight but keeps it active. We'll retreat to the safe f8-square, and the bishop on f4 is still under attack.} 9...Kf8 10.Be3 {The e3-square is not the best one.} 10...Bb4 {We can keep the pressure with ...Bb4, rather than trade the bishops. Our idea is ...d5 and ...Bxf5, while the knight on c3 is pinned. White's aggression is fading away.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Main Line Cobra (6.Ndb5) / Study 1: White plays 7.Nd6+ / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/a5585b11-3230-4007-80fb-9b0102a2c767/studies/10806b6b-67bf-4f0f-bbf7-6c0b27218d20/lines"]

1.e4 {Having considered some of the most popular sixth moves at the amateur level, let's focus on the most common move at master level, 6.Nbd5, which is the Cobra variation. Instead of trading the knights or developing the dark-squared bishops, White takes control of the d6-square immediately with the knight. We will have to implement a very unconventional approach against this.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates a knight. Let's do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {Going for central expansion. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop both knights now.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends the pawn. Preparing ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Ndb5 {The Cobra variation. White wants to get to the d6-square quickly, and we can't play ...d5 to stop it (there are Nc7+ tactics eventually). At the amateur level, this move only happens 8% of the time, which is still almost 400,000 games. In fact, let's develop the dark-squared bishop!} 6...Bc5 7.Nd6+ {In Study 1, we will analyze White checking us on d6, which happens more than 70% of the time. We played ...Bc5 so that we can capture on d6 once the knight arrives there, right? Nope! We do not want to trade our dark-squared bishop but rather claim that our king will be safe on e7. In fact, White's knight is the one under attack.} 7...Ke7 8.Bf4 {The best move for White is 8.Bf4, and it's played 28% of the time. It's best for White to hold on to the d6-square a while longer. We'll chase the pieces away, but at the cost of the d5-square. White has to react now as both the bishop and the knight are under pressure.} 8...e5 9.Nf5+ {A move that I'd say makes more sense than trading on c8 is Nf5+. White saves the knight but keeps it active. We'll retreat to the safe f8-square, and the bishop on f4 is still under attack.} 9...Kf8 10.Bg5 {A more dangerous alternative than Be3 is White creating this pin and fighting for control of the vulnerable d5-square. We will play ...d6 and allow their knight to come to d5 if that's what they want, opening the e6-square for our bishop with combined ideas of ...h6 and ...g6. This also sets a trap.} 10...d6 11.Bc4 {White can develop the bishop to c4 here. Sensible. We'll offer a trade right away. If White takes, their knight on f5 will be under attack.} 11...Be6 12.Bb3 {They retreat to b3 instead. That gives us some momentum and time. To kick away their bishop on g5.} 12...h6 13.Bxf6 {White captures.} 13...Qxf6 {And we'll recapture with the queen and reach a complex position. On the queenside, a good plan is to put the rook on the b-file and push ...a5 and ...b5. On the kingside, we can consider ...g6 to kick the White knight away and ...Ne7 at some point. We do have to expect a knight to arrive on d5, in which case we should have the d8-square free for the queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Main Line Cobra (6.Ndb5) / Study 1: White plays 7.Nd6+ / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/a5585b11-3230-4007-80fb-9b0102a2c767/studies/10806b6b-67bf-4f0f-bbf7-6c0b27218d20/lines"]

1.e4 {Having considered some of the most popular sixth moves at the amateur level, let's focus on the most common move at master level, 6.Nbd5, which is the Cobra variation. Instead of trading the knights or developing the dark-squared bishops, White takes control of the d6-square immediately with the knight. We will have to implement a very unconventional approach against this.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates a knight. Let's do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {Going for central expansion. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop both knights now.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends the pawn. Preparing ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Ndb5 {The Cobra variation. White wants to get to the d6-square quickly, and we can't play ...d5 to stop it (there are Nc7+ tactics eventually). At the amateur level, this move only happens 8% of the time, which is still almost 400,000 games. In fact, let's develop the dark-squared bishop!} 6...Bc5 7.Nd6+ {In Study 1, we will analyze White checking us on d6, which happens more than 70% of the time. We played ...Bc5 so that we can capture on d6 once the knight arrives there, right? Nope! We do not want to trade our dark-squared bishop but rather claim that our king will be safe on e7. In fact, White's knight is the one under attack.} 7...Ke7 8.Nc4 {8.Nc4 is impractical as White is losing a lot of tempi while we're developing and improving. It's time to crush them. We'll play aggressively with our lead in development.} 8...d5 9.exd5 {Taking in the center loses the game already. We'll recapture and open up our bishop. White still has to worry about the knight, while we have ideas such as ...Qb6, ...Re8 and ...Kf8.} 9...exd5 10.Nd2 {If White retreats to d2, it's even worse.} 10...Ng4 {Because we get ...Ng4 and are converging on the f2-pawn. An easy win.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Main Line Cobra (6.Ndb5) / Study 2: White plays 7.Bf4, 7.Bg5 or 7.Be3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/a5585b11-3230-4007-80fb-9b0102a2c767/studies/97f2c2b0-2080-4a5b-94a4-56c4531760b6/lines"]

1.e4 {The most common move at amateur level is 7.Nd6+, the tempting check on the d6-square, and it is by no means a blunder. However, the moves often differ by popularity when we consider the master level, and the most popular move at that level is 7.Bf4, with White dominating the dark squares even more. Apart from that move, this study will deal with 7.Bg5 and 7.Be3.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. We'll develop it as well.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White expands in the center. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop a knight as well.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. Let's go for the standard ...e6.} 5...e6 6.Ndb5 {White plays the Cobra. We'll develop the bishop. As mentioned, White doesn't have to give the check on d6.} 6...Bc5 7.Bf4 {It's only played 16% of the time among amateurs, but 7.Bf4 is certainly a potent option. White's control of the dark squares around our king is concerning. However, we will be fine if we tread carefully. Let's castle and avoid any forks on the c7-square.} 7...O-O 8.Bd6 {The most common move in this position, played 38% of the time, is 8.Bd6. White wants to trade the bishops and establish a strong piece on d6. We won't make it that easy. Let's create a battery and attack f2.} 8...Qb6 9.Bxc5 {White eliminates the threat. We'll recapture.} 9...Qxc5 10.Qd6 {You can expect another trade offer from White on d6. We don't gain anything by trading here. It's better to slide the queen back to b6 and attack both f2 and perhaps b2 in the future.} 10...Qb6 11.Qc7 {White offers a trade of queens again. Now we can draw the game by bringing the queen back to c5, but we don't want that! Let's go for the healthy and active alternative instead: ...d5. We allow White to trade the queens but rely on our central presence and activity to potentially obtain an advantage.} 11...d5 12.Qxb6 {White can't wait to trade. We'll recapture.} 12...axb6 13.exd5 {A trade of pawns in the center occurs as well. Let's recapture.} 13...exd5 14.Nc7 {White can try to fork our rook and the pawn on d5.} 14...Ra5 {But we have a defensive measure with ...Ra5. Now we have some prospects of ...d4 together with ...Bf5 to kick away White's knight and attack the c2-pawn. The position is equal but there's a lot of chess left to be played.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Main Line Cobra (6.Ndb5) / Study 2: White plays 7.Bf4, 7.Bg5 or 7.Be3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/a5585b11-3230-4007-80fb-9b0102a2c767/studies/97f2c2b0-2080-4a5b-94a4-56c4531760b6/lines"]

1.e4 {The most common move at amateur level is 7.Nd6+, the tempting check on the d6-square, and it is by no means a blunder. However, the moves often differ by popularity when we consider the master level, and the most popular move at that level is 7.Bf4, with White dominating the dark squares even more. Apart from that move, this study will deal with 7.Bg5 and 7.Be3.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. We'll develop it as well.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White expands in the center. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop a knight as well.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. Let's go for the standard ...e6.} 5...e6 6.Ndb5 {White plays the Cobra. We'll develop the bishop. As mentioned, White doesn't have to give the check on d6.} 6...Bc5 7.Bf4 {It's only played 16% of the time among amateurs, but 7.Bf4 is certainly a potent option. White's control of the dark squares around our king is concerning. However, we will be fine if we tread carefully. Let's castle and avoid any forks on the c7-square.} 7...O-O 8.Bc7 {The best move for White is the scary-looking Bc7, attacking our queen. You can expect to see it 28% of the time. What's the point of this move?} 8...Qe7 9.Bd6 {Well, White plays Bd6 now! The difference between this line and the main line is that we don't have the option of playing ...Qb6, thanks to White's clever Bc7 idea. We have to trade now.} 9...Bxd6 10.Qxd6 {The queen recaptures. We have no hope for an advantage if we trade the queens, too. Let's keep them on the board and slide the queen to d8. We will slowly improve from here.} 10...Qd8 11.O-O-O {White castles long. Let's attack the knight first.} 11...a6 12.Nd4 {Once it jumps to the center, we will avoid a trade once again.} 12...Ne7 {We will retreat to e7, and here are our ideas. We'll expand with ...b5 and develop the bishop to b7 at some point. We can increase our activity in the center by retreating to e8 and then pushing ...d5.} *

