1.e4 {The most common move at amateur level is 7.Nd6+, the tempting check on the d6-square, and it is by no means a blunder. However, the moves often differ by popularity when we consider the master level, and the most popular move at that level is 7.Bf4, with White dominating the dark squares even more. Apart from that move, this study will deal with 7.Bg5 and 7.Be3.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. We'll develop it as well.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White expands in the center. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop a knight as well.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. Let's go for the standard ...e6.} 5...e6 6.Ndb5 {White plays the Cobra. We'll develop the bishop. As mentioned, White doesn't have to give the check on d6.} 6...Bc5 7.Bf4 {It's only played 16% of the time among amateurs, but 7.Bf4 is certainly a potent option. White's control of the dark squares around our king is concerning. However, we will be fine if we tread carefully. Let's castle and avoid any forks on the c7-square.} 7...O-O 8.Nc7 {This doesn't work now. And it's played 12% of the time.} 8...e5 {White simply loses material.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Main Line Cobra (6.Ndb5) / Study 2: White plays 7.Bf4, 7.Bg5 or 7.Be3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/a5585b11-3230-4007-80fb-9b0102a2c767/studies/97f2c2b0-2080-4a5b-94a4-56c4531760b6/lines"]

1.e4 {The most common move at amateur level is 7.Nd6+, the tempting check on the d6-square, and it is by no means a blunder. However, the moves often differ by popularity when we consider the master level, and the most popular move at that level is 7.Bf4, with White dominating the dark squares even more. Apart from that move, this study will deal with 7.Bg5 and 7.Be3.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. We'll develop it as well.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White expands in the center. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop a knight as well.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. Let's go for the standard ...e6.} 5...e6 6.Ndb5 {White plays the Cobra. We'll develop the bishop. As mentioned, White doesn't have to give the check on d6.} 6...Bc5 7.Be3 {White might think of Be3 to try and invade on d6, but we are not worried about that at all. This mistake is played only 3% of the time, though. We'll take on e3. This will ruin White's pawn structure eventually.} 7...Bxe3 8.Nd6+ {They can throw in this check. But we are not afraid of placing our king on e7.} 8...Ke7 9.fxe3 {The pawn recaptures, and White's pawns on the e-file definitely aren't a beautiful sight to see.} 9...Qc7 {9...Qc7 gives us a clear advantage. White can't hold the knight on d6. For example, 10.Nc4 a6! prevents Nb5, and we can continue with ...Rd8, ...Kf8, or maybe even ...d5. If 10.Ndb5, we have 10...Qe5 with a powerful queen and a great structure. 10.Ncb5 Qb6.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Main Line Cobra (6.Ndb5) / Study 2: White plays 7.Bf4, 7.Bg5 or 7.Be3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/a5585b11-3230-4007-80fb-9b0102a2c767/studies/97f2c2b0-2080-4a5b-94a4-56c4531760b6/lines"]

1.e4 {The most common move at amateur level is 7.Nd6+, the tempting check on the d6-square, and it is by no means a blunder. However, the moves often differ by popularity when we consider the master level, and the most popular move at that level is 7.Bf4, with White dominating the dark squares even more. Apart from that move, this study will deal with 7.Bg5 and 7.Be3.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. We'll develop it as well.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White expands in the center. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop a knight as well.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. Let's go for the standard ...e6.} 5...e6 6.Ndb5 {White plays the Cobra. We'll develop the bishop. As mentioned, White doesn't have to give the check on d6.} 6...Bc5 7.Bg5 {7.Bg5 is only played 5% of the time, and doesn't have real independent value.} 7...O-O {We can just castle with natural plans of ...a6, ...d6 and ...Qb6. White's bishop on g5 doesn't accord with their Cobra setup.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 6 Sidelines / Study 1: The Tricky 6.Be2 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/48bc3893-c3ce-45b1-b9a2-2382142ebdb9/studies/c91922a6-f571-4ba5-b9d2-5ae6d001b292/lines"]

1.e4 {This study will focus on another 6th move alternative for White, which is 6.Be2. I call it tricky because it seems like a calm and normal developing move, but White often follows it up with a vicious pawn sacrifice! Let's take a look.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. We'll do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. The standard ...e6.} 5...e6 6.Be2 {White activates the bishop 7% of the time, and it was played in more than 310,000 games. This is a natural developing move after all, and whether it's good or bad will depend on White's follow-up. Let's pin the knight on c3. Now the e4-pawn is under pressure and White has to make an important decision regarding this pawn.} 6...Bb4 7.O-O {A move that's played 18% of the time but has by far the most independent value is 7.O-O. It's actually best for White to sacrifice the pawn on e4! They will look for compensation in activity and the strong bishops. We will take them up on their offer.} 7...Bxc3 8.bxc3 {The pawn recaptures. And we'll grab a pawn in the center. Let's see White's options on how to approach this position where they've sacrificed a pawn.} 8...Nxe4 9.Bd3 {White can also play the dangerous Bd3. Don't go pawn grabbing here and run into Qg4. It's much more important to develop and consolidate after already winning a pawn. Let's protect the knight instead.} 9...d5 10.Ba3 {White prevents castling for a while. It's important to waste no time here but try to castle as quickly as possible. We can attack that bishop immediately, as well as the pawn.} 10...Qa5 11.Qc1 {If the queen defends the bishop...} 11...Nxc3 {Now we can grab another pawn. We are up two pawns, but White has the bishop pair and we can't castle for the moment. An unclear position.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 6 Sidelines / Study 1: The Tricky 6.Be2 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/48bc3893-c3ce-45b1-b9a2-2382142ebdb9/studies/c91922a6-f571-4ba5-b9d2-5ae6d001b292/lines"]

1.e4 {This study will focus on another 6th move alternative for White, which is 6.Be2. I call it tricky because it seems like a calm and normal developing move, but White often follows it up with a vicious pawn sacrifice! Let's take a look.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. We'll do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. The standard ...e6.} 5...e6 6.Be2 {White activates the bishop 7% of the time, and it was played in more than 310,000 games. This is a natural developing move after all, and whether it's good or bad will depend on White's follow-up. Let's pin the knight on c3. Now the e4-pawn is under pressure and White has to make an important decision regarding this pawn.} 6...Bb4 7.O-O {A move that's played 18% of the time but has by far the most independent value is 7.O-O. It's actually best for White to sacrifice the pawn on e4! They will look for compensation in activity and the strong bishops. We will take them up on their offer.} 7...Bxc3 8.bxc3 {The pawn recaptures. And we'll grab a pawn in the center. Let's see White's options on how to approach this position where they've sacrificed a pawn.} 8...Nxe4 9.Bd3 {White can also play the dangerous Bd3. Don't go pawn grabbing here and run into Qg4. It's much more important to develop and consolidate after already winning a pawn. Let's protect the knight instead.} 9...d5 10.Ba3 {White prevents castling for a while. It's important to waste no time here but try to castle as quickly as possible. We can attack that bishop immediately, as well as the pawn.} 10...Qa5 11.Nb5 {White can try to defend with the knight, but that knight is overloaded. One of our options is 11...a6 which eventually leads to a draw by repetition. We can also capture on c3 and threaten to take the bishop if White captures the knight.} 11...Nxc3 12.Nd6+ {But they have an alternative, and that's to give a check on d6. Our king has to move. The d7-square is best.} 12...Kd7 13.Qc1 {Finally, White protects the bishop.} 13...e5 {If 14.Nxf7, ...e4 causes White practical problems since their bishop defends against a fork on e2. 14.Re1 f6 is solid. In general, we plan to build a defensive pawn chain as a substitute for being unable to castle, and we have to look for either an ...e4 or ...d4 pawn push at the right moment.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 6 Sidelines / Study 1: The Tricky 6.Be2 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/48bc3893-c3ce-45b1-b9a2-2382142ebdb9/studies/c91922a6-f571-4ba5-b9d2-5ae6d001b292/lines"]

1.e4 {This study will focus on another 6th move alternative for White, which is 6.Be2. I call it tricky because it seems like a calm and normal developing move, but White often follows it up with a vicious pawn sacrifice! Let's take a look.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. We'll do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. The standard ...e6.} 5...e6 6.Be2 {White activates the bishop 7% of the time, and it was played in more than 310,000 games. This is a natural developing move after all, and whether it's good or bad will depend on White's follow-up. Let's pin the knight on c3. Now the e4-pawn is under pressure and White has to make an important decision regarding this pawn.} 6...Bb4 7.O-O {A move that's played 18% of the time but has by far the most independent value is 7.O-O. It's actually best for White to sacrifice the pawn on e4! They will look for compensation in activity and the strong bishops. We will take them up on their offer.} 7...Bxc3 8.bxc3 {The pawn recaptures. And we'll grab a pawn in the center. Let's see White's options on how to approach this position where they've sacrificed a pawn.} 8...Nxe4 9.Nxc6 {White can trade on c6 now. It's different when the center is open and I advise against recapturing with the b-pawn. White would have the strong Qd4. Let's take with the d-pawn instead and offer a trade of queens.} 9...dxc6 10.Qxd8+ {Trading the queens is good for us because there is less danger and activity for White. We keep the extra pawn and White has to worry about the doubled c-pawns. Even at the cost of losing castling rights.} 10...Kxd8 11.Bf3 {White improves the bishop to f3, attacking our knight. We shouldn't rush with capturing on c3, since Bb2 is powerful. Let's retreat to f6 instead. The knight can maneuver to d5.} 11...Nf6 12.Rd1+ {White gives a check. Nothing wrong in blocking with the bishop.} 12...Bd7 13.Bf4 {As mentioned, White is banking on their strong bishops. We'll attack one of them immediately.} 13...Nd5 14.Bg3 {White retreats to g3. Again, capturing on c3 isn't necessary here. It's better to try and keep our knight on d5 instead.} 14...b5 {This means that ...b5 is useful to prevent c4. We'll connect the rooks with ...Ke7 next and figure out how to play against White's bishops and reach an endgame. All in all, an equal position.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 6 Sidelines / Study 1: The Tricky 6.Be2 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/48bc3893-c3ce-45b1-b9a2-2382142ebdb9/studies/c91922a6-f571-4ba5-b9d2-5ae6d001b292/lines"]

1.e4 {This study will focus on another 6th move alternative for White, which is 6.Be2. I call it tricky because it seems like a calm and normal developing move, but White often follows it up with a vicious pawn sacrifice! Let's take a look.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. We'll do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. The standard ...e6.} 5...e6 6.Be2 {White activates the bishop 7% of the time, and it was played in more than 310,000 games. This is a natural developing move after all, and whether it's good or bad will depend on White's follow-up. Let's pin the knight on c3. Now the e4-pawn is under pressure and White has to make an important decision regarding this pawn.} 6...Bb4 7.Nxc6 {White can choose to trade here, similar to the main line of the Four Knights Sicilian. This happens 25% of the time. We'll recapture with the b-pawn.} 7...bxc6 8.e5 {The idea is e5. This time we can jump to e4 when our bishop is already on b4.} 8...Ne4 9.Qd4 {Did White trick us after Qd4? Both of our pieces are under attack.} 9...Qa5 {We can defend the bishop and add an attacker to the knight. If White captures on e4, we will capture on c3. Otherwise there is a lot of pressure and the e5-pawn is under attack as well. A sharp game to come.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 6 Sidelines / Study 1: The Tricky 6.Be2 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/48bc3893-c3ce-45b1-b9a2-2382142ebdb9/studies/c91922a6-f571-4ba5-b9d2-5ae6d001b292/lines"]

1.e4 {This study will focus on another 6th move alternative for White, which is 6.Be2. I call it tricky because it seems like a calm and normal developing move, but White often follows it up with a vicious pawn sacrifice! Let's take a look.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. We'll do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. The standard ...e6.} 5...e6 6.Be2 {White activates the bishop 7% of the time, and it was played in more than 310,000 games. This is a natural developing move after all, and whether it's good or bad will depend on White's follow-up. Let's pin the knight on c3. Now the e4-pawn is under pressure and White has to make an important decision regarding this pawn.} 6...Bb4 7.Nxc6 {White can choose to trade here, similar to the main line of the Four Knights Sicilian. This happens 25% of the time. We'll recapture with the b-pawn.} 7...bxc6 8.e5 {The idea is e5. This time we can jump to e4 when our bishop is already on b4.} 8...Ne4 9.Bd2 {If the bishop blocks, we will gladly capture it. And remove the bishop pair.} 9...Nxd2 10.Qxd2 {The queen recaptures.} 10...f6 {And now we can begin to challenge White's structure, with castling in mind. 11.exf6 Qxf6 and our queen is out. White lacks central pawns in this variation, which means we'll control the center after we play ...d5.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 6 Sidelines / Study 1: The Tricky 6.Be2 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/48bc3893-c3ce-45b1-b9a2-2382142ebdb9/studies/c91922a6-f571-4ba5-b9d2-5ae6d001b292/lines"]

1.e4 {This study will focus on another 6th move alternative for White, which is 6.Be2. I call it tricky because it seems like a calm and normal developing move, but White often follows it up with a vicious pawn sacrifice! Let's take a look.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. We'll do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. The standard ...e6.} 5...e6 6.Be2 {White activates the bishop 7% of the time, and it was played in more than 310,000 games. This is a natural developing move after all, and whether it's good or bad will depend on White's follow-up. Let's pin the knight on c3. Now the e4-pawn is under pressure and White has to make an important decision regarding this pawn.} 6...Bb4 7.O-O {A move that's played 18% of the time but has by far the most independent value is 7.O-O. It's actually best for White to sacrifice the pawn on e4! They will look for compensation in activity and the strong bishops. We will take them up on their offer.} 7...Bxc3 8.bxc3 {The pawn recaptures. And we'll grab a pawn in the center. Let's see White's options on how to approach this position where they've sacrificed a pawn.} 8...Nxe4 9.Nxc6 {White can trade on c6 now. It's different when the center is open and I advise against recapturing with the b-pawn. White would have the strong Qd4. Let's take with the d-pawn instead and offer a trade of queens.} 9...dxc6 10.Bd3 {White can decline and place the bishop on d3. Let's rotate the knight to c5 and prepare castling. We would like to capture on d3 at some point.} 10...Nc5 11.Qg4 {It's dangerous to do it now if White plays Qg4. The g7-pawn is under attack.} 11...O-O {We can castle instead and protect it. White's bishops do have a lot of potential for now, but once we play ...e5 to expand and attack the queen, a lot can change.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 6 Sidelines / Study 1: The Tricky 6.Be2 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/48bc3893-c3ce-45b1-b9a2-2382142ebdb9/studies/c91922a6-f571-4ba5-b9d2-5ae6d001b292/lines"]

1.e4 {This study will focus on another 6th move alternative for White, which is 6.Be2. I call it tricky because it seems like a calm and normal developing move, but White often follows it up with a vicious pawn sacrifice! Let's take a look.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. We'll do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. The standard ...e6.} 5...e6 6.Be2 {White activates the bishop 7% of the time, and it was played in more than 310,000 games. This is a natural developing move after all, and whether it's good or bad will depend on White's follow-up. Let's pin the knight on c3. Now the e4-pawn is under pressure and White has to make an important decision regarding this pawn.} 6...Bb4 7.O-O {A move that's played 18% of the time but has by far the most independent value is 7.O-O. It's actually best for White to sacrifice the pawn on e4! They will look for compensation in activity and the strong bishops. We will take them up on their offer.} 7...Bxc3 8.bxc3 {The pawn recaptures. And we'll grab a pawn in the center. Let's see White's options on how to approach this position where they've sacrificed a pawn.} 8...Nxe4 9.Qd3 {The most common move at this point is Qd3. White attacks our knight on e4 and defends the c3-pawn. It's much more active to defend the knight with ...d5 than move it away.} 9...d5 10.Ba3 {Don't be scared of Ba3. White won't prevent us from castling for a long time.} 10...Ne7 {We can block the bishop immediately and castle on the next move. White might want c4 at some point, which you shouldn't be afraid of. Letting White capture and playing with an IQP is fine. Of course, ...Re8 is one short-term goal to unpin the knight on e7.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 6 Sidelines / Study 1: The Tricky 6.Be2 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/48bc3893-c3ce-45b1-b9a2-2382142ebdb9/studies/c91922a6-f571-4ba5-b9d2-5ae6d001b292/lines"]

1.e4 {This study will focus on another 6th move alternative for White, which is 6.Be2. I call it tricky because it seems like a calm and normal developing move, but White often follows it up with a vicious pawn sacrifice! Let's take a look.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. We'll do the same.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. The standard ...e6.} 5...e6 6.Be2 {White activates the bishop 7% of the time, and it was played in more than 310,000 games. This is a natural developing move after all, and whether it's good or bad will depend on White's follow-up. Let's pin the knight on c3. Now the e4-pawn is under pressure and White has to make an important decision regarding this pawn.} 6...Bb4 7.f3 {7.f3 is played 24% of the time and runs into ...d5.} 7...d5 {We get to expand in the center, unbothered. 8.exd5 Nxd5 puts pressure on c3 and we have a small edge. We also have ...Qa5 ideas, and we enjoy a comfortable position. White has to be careful not to lose a pawn!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 6 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4, 6.a3 or 6.g3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/48bc3893-c3ce-45b1-b9a2-2382142ebdb9/studies/29548a3c-338c-4a5f-abf5-5947263f77df/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to look at the rarest of sidelines White can choose to play on move 6, which are 6.Bc4, 6.a3 and 6.g3. These are far from optimal and we should get good positions by sticking to basic principles we've learned in this course. If anything, this will be a display of why and how the recommended moves are exceptionally strong against the "bad" sidelines.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight comes out to f3. We'll develop our knight, too.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's capture that pawn.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {The knight comes out to c3 and defends. We'll play ...e6 with the standard plans ahead.} 5...e6 6.a3 {6.a3 is only played 3% of the time, probably because it's slow. However, it does take care of ...Bb4. The direct execution of our main idea is ...d5, so that's the simplest continuation here.} 6...d5 7.exd5 {It's natural for White to take in the center. If we recapture with the pawn, we'll get an IQP. So let's recapture with the knight instead, playing actively!} 7...Nxd5 8.Nxd5 {White takes. Now we'll finally recapture with the queen. It's not good for White to trade off their active knight.} 8...Qxd5 9.Be3 {So they protect the knight instead. Here, we can develop with ...Be7 or ...Bd7 and it's a fine position. Also good, but a little boring, is the next line, capturing in the center. But we will show that line as well.} 9...Nxd4 10.Qxd4 {White recaptures with the queen. We'll trade.} 10...Qxd4 11.Bxd4 {The bishop recaptures.} 11...Bd7 {And now we can bring our bishop to d7 with the idea of ...Bc6, where the bishop will be strong. White's bishop on d4 is good, but we can neutralize it with ...Rg8. Both sides have a pawn majority they can use in the endgame.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 6 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4, 6.a3 or 6.g3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/48bc3893-c3ce-45b1-b9a2-2382142ebdb9/studies/29548a3c-338c-4a5f-abf5-5947263f77df/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to look at the rarest of sidelines White can choose to play on move 6, which are 6.Bc4, 6.a3 and 6.g3. These are far from optimal and we should get good positions by sticking to basic principles we've learned in this course. If anything, this will be a display of why and how the recommended moves are exceptionally strong against the "bad" sidelines.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight comes out to f3. We'll develop our knight, too.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's capture that pawn.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {The knight comes out to c3 and defends. We'll play ...e6 with the standard plans ahead.} 5...e6 6.Bc4 {The c4-square is simply a silly square for the bishop, as it directly runs into all of our ideas. You will face this move 5% of the time, and it was played in just under 250,000 games. We'll pin the knight first, reintroducing the threat on e4 with the idea of ...d5, of course.} 6...Bb4 7.Nxc6 {White takes on c6 first. We'll recapture with the b-pawn, as per usual.} 7...bxc6 8.e5 {White can attack our knight, but it's slightly different than the main lines. We can utilize the pin on c3 and prepare to bring the queen to a5 to help out with the attack. White's best move is Qg4 here.} 8...Ne4 9.Bd2 {For example, Bd2 is a blunder, although it seems natural. We can capture on f2!} 9...Nxf2 10.Kxf2 {White takes.} 10...Qh4+ {And we have the fork on h4, taking advantage of the misplaced bishop on c4. A winning position for us.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 6 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4, 6.a3 or 6.g3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/48bc3893-c3ce-45b1-b9a2-2382142ebdb9/studies/29548a3c-338c-4a5f-abf5-5947263f77df/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to look at the rarest of sidelines White can choose to play on move 6, which are 6.Bc4, 6.a3 and 6.g3. These are far from optimal and we should get good positions by sticking to basic principles we've learned in this course. If anything, this will be a display of why and how the recommended moves are exceptionally strong against the "bad" sidelines.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight comes out to f3. We'll develop our knight, too.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's capture that pawn.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {The knight comes out to c3 and defends. We'll play ...e6 with the standard plans ahead.} 5...e6 6.Bc4 {The c4-square is simply a silly square for the bishop, as it directly runs into all of our ideas. You will face this move 5% of the time, and it was played in just under 250,000 games. We'll pin the knight first, reintroducing the threat on e4 with the idea of ...d5, of course.} 6...Bb4 7.Nxc6 {White takes on c6 first. We'll recapture with the b-pawn, as per usual.} 7...bxc6 8.Bd2 {White can defend with the bishop. Our plans stay the same. We'll strike in the center. White is facing the typical central problems.} 8...d5 9.exd5 {White takes once.} 9...cxd5 {We'll recapture with the c-pawn and keep our powerful pawn chain in the center. If White gives a check on b5, we will block with the bishop. Otherwise, castling and ...Bb7 is strong. White gave us everything we wanted.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 6 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4, 6.a3 or 6.g3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/48bc3893-c3ce-45b1-b9a2-2382142ebdb9/studies/29548a3c-338c-4a5f-abf5-5947263f77df/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to look at the rarest of sidelines White can choose to play on move 6, which are 6.Bc4, 6.a3 and 6.g3. These are far from optimal and we should get good positions by sticking to basic principles we've learned in this course. If anything, this will be a display of why and how the recommended moves are exceptionally strong against the "bad" sidelines.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight comes out to f3. We'll develop our knight, too.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's capture that pawn.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {The knight comes out to c3 and defends. We'll play ...e6 with the standard plans ahead.} 5...e6 6.g3 {This option is not bad compared to White's other moves and some players enjoy playing fianchetto ideas. Here, it's only played 1% of the time, though. Still, it does nothing to stop our plan in the center.} 6...d5 7.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture. We now have an IQP.} 7...exd5 8.Bg2 {Which is convenient for White to attack with Bg2. Most of the play will revolve around this pawn. But we have a tricky move now. We can activate the bishop to g4 with tempo!} 8...Bg4 9.Qd3 {White escapes to d3. They had to keep an eye on the knight on d4. Let's put more pressure on it now. We are happy to see White trade on c6 at any point.} 9...Bc5 10.Be3 {Instead, they can choose to keep the knight on d4 and continue developing. We will insist. The queen is attacking the b2-pawn as well.} 10...Qb6 11.Nxc6 {White is basically forced to trade now. I suggest we take on e3 first. We don't want to allow White to trade on c5.} 11...Bxe3 12.Qxe3+ {The queen recaptures. The whole point of these trades is to create a big weakness.} 12...Qxe3+ 13.fxe3 {White recaptures.} 13...bxc6 {Now our pawns are back together and White has an obvious weakness on e3. We will play against this target next. I'm sure this is not what White had hoped for at the beginning of the game.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 6 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4, 6.a3 or 6.g3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/48bc3893-c3ce-45b1-b9a2-2382142ebdb9/studies/29548a3c-338c-4a5f-abf5-5947263f77df/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to look at the rarest of sidelines White can choose to play on move 6, which are 6.Bc4, 6.a3 and 6.g3. These are far from optimal and we should get good positions by sticking to basic principles we've learned in this course. If anything, this will be a display of why and how the recommended moves are exceptionally strong against the "bad" sidelines.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight comes out to f3. We'll develop our knight, too.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's capture that pawn.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {The knight comes out to c3 and defends. We'll play ...e6 with the standard plans ahead.} 5...e6 6.g3 {This option is not bad compared to White's other moves and some players enjoy playing fianchetto ideas. Here, it's only played 1% of the time, though. Still, it does nothing to stop our plan in the center.} 6...d5 7.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture. We now have an IQP.} 7...exd5 8.Bg2 {Which is convenient for White to attack with Bg2. Most of the play will revolve around this pawn. But we have a tricky move now. We can activate the bishop to g4 with tempo!} 8...Bg4 9.f3 {Provoking f3 is basically what we wanted. We'll retreat, and now White's bishop isn't active anymore.} 9...Bd7 10.O-O {So they resort to castling. Let's develop the other bishop as well, preparing castling ourselves.} 10...Be7 11.Re1 {White pins our bishop.} 11...O-O {And we'll castle, with active plans ahead. The rooks are coming to e8 and c8 and the bishop will improve to either c5 or b4. We should focus on protecting the IQP in the long run.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 6 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4, 6.a3 or 6.g3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/48bc3893-c3ce-45b1-b9a2-2382142ebdb9/studies/29548a3c-338c-4a5f-abf5-5947263f77df/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to look at the rarest of sidelines White can choose to play on move 6, which are 6.Bc4, 6.a3 and 6.g3. These are far from optimal and we should get good positions by sticking to basic principles we've learned in this course. If anything, this will be a display of why and how the recommended moves are exceptionally strong against the "bad" sidelines.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight comes out to f3. We'll develop our knight, too.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. Let's capture that pawn.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {The knight comes out to c3 and defends. We'll play ...e6 with the standard plans ahead.} 5...e6 6.a3 {6.a3 is only played 3% of the time, probably because it's slow. However, it does take care of ...Bb4. The direct execution of our main idea is ...d5, so that's the simplest continuation here.} 6...d5 7.Bb5 {You could also expect Bb5 at a certain frequency. White players love to pin the knights like this when there's already tension between them. We'll simply unpin and defend.} 7...Bd7 8.exd5 {White captures in the center once. We'll take back.} 8...exd5 9.O-O {White castles. We can prepare to do the same. A calm game.} 9...Be7 10.Re1 {The rook pins our bishop on the e-file.} 10...O-O {But we can unpin immediately by castling. Having almost finished development, it's time to improve next. We'll do that with ...a6, ...Re8 and ...Rc8. Both sides have easy development although White is slightly more active.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 5 Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 5.Nxc6 or 5.f3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/88ce8dfa-004c-4838-af2e-139f7fedf243/studies/b82a8b27-9b91-4934-840b-c9f7e5cf6cd2/lines"]

1.e4 {In the sixth chapter of this course, we will analyze White's sidelines already on move 5. The most logical move is of course 5.Nc3 to defend the e4-pawn, but what if White doesn't play that? Viable options could also be 5.Nxc6 and 5.f3. Let's see.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The standard continuation. We'll get our piece out, too.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. We'll take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the pawn. All standard so far.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxc6 {We are generally happy to see this trade because we can strengthen our center and open up the b-file. 5.Nxc6 is played 20% of the time, and it was seen in more than 2 million games! So we'll recapture with the b-pawn.} 5...bxc6 6.Nc3 {White defends the pawn now almost 45% of the time. But one can argue that the trade on c6 was unnecessary if this was their plan all along. So we'll strike in the center.} 6...d5 7.e5 {White can try to push here. We'll attack the pawn. They can't play e6 like in the 6.Bd3 line because there is no bishop on d3.} 7...Nd7 8.Bf4 {Defending with the bishop is not the best option. It's going to be passive in the long run and it leaves the defense of the b2-pawn. We can block the pawn first and prepare to harass b2 with the queen at some point.} 8...e6 9.Bd3 {White brings the bishop to d3. Before anything else, we can demonstrate how to make use of the b-file.} 9...Rb8 10.Rb1 {White defends. But they will have to react one more time because we are attacking the pawn on e5.} 10...Qc7 11.Qe2 {They defend with the queen. Our queen is convenient on c7 because we can target the pawn with ...f6. White can't capture because the pawn is pinned.} 11...f6 12.Qh5+ {They can give this check.} 12...Kd8 {But our king is actually safe on d8 and now White can't save the pawn on e5. We'll take it with the knight and already reach an advantage that can't be dismissed. This whole line was us targeting weaknesses and White defending. That's what happens when you don't play your opening in the best way.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 5 Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 5.Nxc6 or 5.f3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/88ce8dfa-004c-4838-af2e-139f7fedf243/studies/b82a8b27-9b91-4934-840b-c9f7e5cf6cd2/lines"]

1.e4 {In the sixth chapter of this course, we will analyze White's sidelines already on move 5. The most logical move is of course 5.Nc3 to defend the e4-pawn, but what if White doesn't play that? Viable options could also be 5.Nxc6 and 5.f3. Let's see.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The standard continuation. We'll get our piece out, too.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. We'll take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the pawn. All standard so far.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxc6 {We are generally happy to see this trade because we can strengthen our center and open up the b-file. 5.Nxc6 is played 20% of the time, and it was seen in more than 2 million games! So we'll recapture with the b-pawn.} 5...bxc6 6.Nc3 {White defends the pawn now almost 45% of the time. But one can argue that the trade on c6 was unnecessary if this was their plan all along. So we'll strike in the center.} 6...d5 7.e5 {White can try to push here. We'll attack the pawn. They can't play e6 like in the 6.Bd3 line because there is no bishop on d3.} 7...Nd7 8.f4 {It's better to protect the pawn with f4. We'll block White's structure.} 8...e6 9.Bd3 {The bishop comes out between the pawns.} 9...a5 {And we'll go ...a5, with a lot of ideas in mind. They are to push ...d4 and/or ...c5 and ...c4. The c5-square is also a good square for the knight. We can trade the light-squared bishops on a6 and play ...Be7 to prepare castling.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 5 Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 5.Nxc6 or 5.f3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/88ce8dfa-004c-4838-af2e-139f7fedf243/studies/b82a8b27-9b91-4934-840b-c9f7e5cf6cd2/lines"]

1.e4 {In the sixth chapter of this course, we will analyze White's sidelines already on move 5. The most logical move is of course 5.Nc3 to defend the e4-pawn, but what if White doesn't play that? Viable options could also be 5.Nxc6 and 5.f3. Let's see.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The standard continuation. We'll get our piece out, too.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. We'll take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the pawn. All standard so far.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxc6 {We are generally happy to see this trade because we can strengthen our center and open up the b-file. 5.Nxc6 is played 20% of the time, and it was seen in more than 2 million games! So we'll recapture with the b-pawn.} 5...bxc6 6.Bd3 {Let's look at a funny line White can go for here. 6.Bd3 is played more than 25% of the time. White is awaiting ...d5 and has a plan. We'll play it. Expanding in the center is essential.} 6...d5 7.e5 {White wanted to push e5 the whole time. We'll retreat to d7 and attack the pawn. What now? Will White play f4 or Bf4 to protect the pawn?} 7...Nd7 8.e6 {Nope! Their whole idea is e6! We can't capture on e6 as that would lead to a quick checkmate for White. Let's bring the knight to e5 instead.} 8...Ne5 9.exf7+ {So that once White captures on f7... We can recapture with the knight.} 9...Nxf7 10.O-O {White castles.} 10...e6 {And we'll play ...e6 ourselves. This is a ridiculous line but I'd say we come out on top with the important central presence. We'll develop naturally next.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 5 Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 5.Nxc6 or 5.f3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/88ce8dfa-004c-4838-af2e-139f7fedf243/studies/b82a8b27-9b91-4934-840b-c9f7e5cf6cd2/lines"]

1.e4 {In the sixth chapter of this course, we will analyze White's sidelines already on move 5. The most logical move is of course 5.Nc3 to defend the e4-pawn, but what if White doesn't play that? Viable options could also be 5.Nxc6 and 5.f3. Let's see.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The standard continuation. We'll get our piece out, too.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. We'll take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the pawn. All standard so far.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxc6 {We are generally happy to see this trade because we can strengthen our center and open up the b-file. 5.Nxc6 is played 20% of the time, and it was seen in more than 2 million games! So we'll recapture with the b-pawn.} 5...bxc6 6.Nc3 {White defends the pawn now almost 45% of the time. But one can argue that the trade on c6 was unnecessary if this was their plan all along. So we'll strike in the center.} 6...d5 7.exd5 {It's much more frequent for White to capture on d5 here than push e5. We'll recapture, of course. When both the b- and the c-pawns are missing, you should expect some pressure on the e8-a4 diagonal.} 7...cxd5 8.Bb5+ {White gives a check on the b5-square. We'll block with the bishop.} 8...Bd7 9.Bxd7+ {White trades. No problem. We'll recapture with the queen.} 9...Qxd7 10.O-O {White castles.} 10...e6 {Pretty standard and easy development for both sides. We connect the pawn chain and prepare ...Be7 and ...O-O. The rook will occupy the semi-open b-file and we'll enjoy a comfortable position. We could even bring the kingside rook to the c-file and dominate the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 5 Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 5.Nxc6 or 5.f3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/88ce8dfa-004c-4838-af2e-139f7fedf243/studies/b82a8b27-9b91-4934-840b-c9f7e5cf6cd2/lines"]

1.e4 {In the sixth chapter of this course, we will analyze White's sidelines already on move 5. The most logical move is of course 5.Nc3 to defend the e4-pawn, but what if White doesn't play that? Viable options could also be 5.Nxc6 and 5.f3. Let's see.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The standard continuation. We'll get our piece out, too.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. We'll take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the pawn. All standard so far.} 4...Nf6 5.f3 {5.f3 is only played 2% of the time here. It does protect the e4-pawn, but doesn't fight for the center. We are ahead in development and are ready to strike with ...d5 and/or develop actively with ...Bc5 to exploit the g1-a7 diagonal. It was also definitely possible to immediately attack with 5...d5!} 5...e6 6.c4 {Even if White goes for c4...} 6...d5 {We can still play ...d5. This will open up the center and give us easy development and activity.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 5 Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 5.Nxc6 or 5.f3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/f748b447-64b2-47c7-9a9e-728304499ee8/chapters/88ce8dfa-004c-4838-af2e-139f7fedf243/studies/b82a8b27-9b91-4934-840b-c9f7e5cf6cd2/lines"]

1.e4 {In the sixth chapter of this course, we will analyze White's sidelines already on move 5. The most logical move is of course 5.Nc3 to defend the e4-pawn, but what if White doesn't play that? Viable options could also be 5.Nxc6 and 5.f3. Let's see.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The standard continuation. We'll get our piece out, too.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White strikes in the center. We'll take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the pawn. All standard so far.} 4...Nf6 5.f3 {5.f3 is only played 2% of the time here. It does protect the e4-pawn, but doesn't fight for the center. We are ahead in development and are ready to strike with ...d5 and/or develop actively with ...Bc5 to exploit the g1-a7 diagonal. It was also definitely possible to immediately attack with 5...d5!} 5...e6 6.Be3 {Let's demonstrate how strong our plans are if White plays a natural but soft move like Be3. We can strike in the center.} 6...d5 7.Nxc6 {Capturing on c6 doesn't really help White.} 7...bxc6 {We can just recapture on c6 and keep our strong pawns in the center. White now has the unnecessary pawn on f3 and we already have a slight advantage.} *